/** Recorded English Web Treebank results and EM histories for the hidden
 * Markov models lesson.
 *
 * Generated by scripts/verify-hmm-data.py, which recomputes the whole declared
 * supervised protocol from the extract this lesson serves - coarse label map,
 * vocabulary, smoothed counts, both decoders, every development prediction and
 * every per-token belief - through an implementation independent of the content
 * packet's author program, matches every value against that packet, and
 * re-derives the packet's own calculated inputs leaf by leaf. Do not edit by hand.
 *
 * Source: Universal Dependencies English Web Treebank r2.16, published May 2025,
 * annotations licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. This lesson serves its own copy of the
 * unchanged extract at /learn-assets/hmm/ewt-sequences.json
 * (89597 bytes, SHA-256 e801e665c4e2a6fa002e04ebb52b00b4fbc1421c7f029494565c0ecbaa016a4d).
 * The full attribution, licence and extraction record is served beside it.
 *
 * Protocol: NOUN and PROPN become Noun, VERB and AUX become Verb, every other
 * original tag becomes Other. Training words are lowercased and retained when they
 * occur at least twice, giving 146 emission symbols including one unknown category;
 * development words never change that vocabulary, and 140 of the 341 development
 * tokens map to unknown. Initial, transition and emission probabilities are
 * smoothed counts at strength 0.1 or 1.0, giving two fits and four decoder
 * configurations. Development token accuracy selects among them; ties prefer the
 * lexical decoder and then the smaller strength. The 40 reserved sentences are
 * never predicted or scored.
 *
 * `emTrack` holds measured calculations rather than real data: twelve length-30
 * recordings sampled from the constructed two-state model with seed 71, and the
 * exact-EM objective after every update for three Dirichlet starts and one uniform
 * start. The browser reads these; it never runs EM while a reader scrolls.
 *
 * Two fitted models serve four configurations: `configurations[i].fitIndex`
 * names the entry of `fittedModels` a decoder used, because the lexical rule
 * and the Viterbi path read the SAME fitted counts. Each sentence's beliefs
 * are one flat row of 3T numbers, read three at a time in Noun, Verb, Other
 * order. Beliefs carry six decimal places, the precision the page prints;
 * probability rows keep twelve and the emission table nine.
 *
 * `tieAudit` records something the float decoders cannot establish about
 * themselves: in exact rational arithmetic, three of the forty development
 * sentences have TWO paths of exactly equal joint probability, so the
 * recorded token counts are one member of a band. The stored paths are
 * proved to be exact maximisers, and the band is reported rather than hidden.
 */

